  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    Silvtasaevus~ shifted the charcoal-wrapped bundle against the terrace wall and the ice wept around it. Not melted—wept. A slow bead tracing down from where the cloth touched crystal, pooling in a scorch-mark older than anyone on the line.
    
    "Your boot," ☽'umbraum said.
    
    "I know about the boot."
    
    "There's a crack in your boot that goes all the way up to your—"
    
    "I *know* about the boot, Umbra."
    
    ☽'umbraum raised both flippers in mock surrender. He was leaning against a shattered column on the second tier, half in shadow, the bioluminescent thread along his cloak-hem tracing his outline like something the dark was trying to keep. "Just saying. When the line breaks, you'll lose that foot first."
    
    "When the line breaks," Silvtasaevus~ said, "my foot won't be the part of me I'm worried about."
    
    Three laugh-sounds. Not actual laughter—the compressed exhalation the Midnight Claws used in place of it, humor processed through too many collapsed tunnels. Velumia~ made the sound from her position near the eastern spire, where she was scratching marks into an ice-ledger with a bone stylus. She was always scratching marks. Debts, rations, who'd taken the last smoked krill. Even now, with the hunt-songs audible three junctions out—a bone-deep vibration that made the terrace's remaining crystal sing in sympathy. As if anyone would read it later.
    
    "Nana." ☽'umbraum's voice, softer. "Tell me those aren't the last fire-cores."
    
    Nanaiamis~ held up the satchel without looking at him. Her flippers shook. They always shook. The squad had a phrase for it—*steady as Nanaiamis~*—which meant visibly broken but somehow still doing the job. "Four full. Two cracked. The cracked ones might hold for a casting, might blow your face off. Exciting either way."
    
    "The math doesn't hold," Augur said.
    
    Everyone looked at the hooded shape by the wall. Augur ☽'Milucaum~ hadn't spoken in two hours. They sat cross-legged near the bundle Silvtasaevus~ hated carrying, the Radiant Ring dull and dead-looking on their right flipper. Their scarred forge-hands—old burns ragged and deep—rested on their knees like they were waiting for instructions from somewhere below hearing.
    
    "Augur speaks," ☽'umbraum said, putting one flipper to his chest. "Note the date, Velumia~. The ledgers require—"
    
    "The math doesn't hold," Augur said again, "and you know it."
    
    "We know lots of things," Nanaiamis~ said. She was touching the fire-core amulet at her throat with one shaking flipper. "Knowing things is our best feature. Right after our looks." She pulled a fire-core from the satchel and turned it in the low light. The aurora had been stuttering for hours, the crystal spires above them flickering in arrhythmic pulses that made shadows lurch. "Silvs, when this is done, you owe me a drink at the Ledge."
    
    "The Ledge got seized."
    
    "Then wherever. Pick a ruin. I'm not particular."
    
    "Later," Silvtasaevus~ said. The promise no one believed. Everyone made it.
    
    "That reminds me of the time at Ravium~—" ☽'umbraum began.
    
    "Don't," Velumia~ said.
    
    "—when I was keeping count—"
    
    "You weren't keeping count, you were hiding behind a mineral pipe."
    
    "I was *strategically positioned* behind a mineral pipe, and—"
    
    "Tell the one about the pipe," Nanaiamis~ said, and ☽'umbraum grinned the way he always grinned, like the world was a joke that kept getting funnier the worse it got.
    
    The crack in the terrace floor whistled when the wind shifted—thin, shrieking, something trapped. Below, the colony's families huddled in the interior spires: nurseries, the old, the ones who couldn't fight. Three terraces had already gone. The footing was wrong where the trade-platform's polished surface began, slick with condensation coming off the bundle against the wall.
    
    The Obelisk. Seven feet of pressure-blackened whale-bone in charcoal cloth that didn't quite contain it. The coral at its base had been extending tendrils across the wet stone since they'd set up, slow blind fingers reaching for the nearest warm body. ☽'umbraum had named it the Bastard. Silvtasaevus~, who carried it, called it nothing.
    
    Nanaiamis~ started to say something about Pactum∴ne—the word coming out the way you'd say the name of a dead relative who'd written you out of the will—about which clauses they were violating just by being here. Dissolved on paper, sentenced to labor-service, ghosts doing one last unauthorized job. But the hunt-songs buried it. The sound rose from the eastern approach like something surfacing, deep enough to make teeth ache.
    
    "That's close," Velumia~ said, her stylus pausing.
    
    "Your powers of observation," ☽'umbraum began, "continue to—the time at Ravium~, when I was the one who—"
    
    The first spire shattered.
    
    ---
    
    Crystal didn't break like ice. It sang—a high shrieking frequency that climbed past hearing into pain, and then the fragments came down like shrapnel, finding gaps in wrappings, exposed patches of skin, the cracked boot. Silvtasaevus~ threw himself flat. Nanaiamis~ was already behind the column. Augur hadn't moved.
    
    "Left!" Velumia~ screamed. "Left, they're coming through the—"
    
    The second spire went. The terrace lurched, the middle tier's scorch-marks cracking open into gaps you could lose a flipper in. Three shapes in the water below—massive, coordinated, moving with the terrible precision of a formation practiced until it was reflex.
    
    Shore-breakers. The impact traveled up through the crystal scaffolding and into every body on it. ☽'umbraum fired a fire-core charge down the approach and the orange light caught them for one heartbeat: scarred fins carved with the pressure-marks of Khrrak's Collapseritual cuts that blurred the line between body and weapon—one orca with a wound along his flank that wept cold-water.
    
    Gnarkzund Zaith And at his shoulder, always at his shoulder, Thung Thrakrekbroad-finned, precise, already reading the terrace's weak points the way Nanaiamis~ read a ledger. When Gnark shifted left, Thung filled the space he'd vacated, the two of them reading each other's movement the way old partners read breath.
    
    The warrior beside Gnarkzund Zaith sang. Not a hunt-song—a coordination tone, three rising notes that told the pod where to push. Two more shapes came through the channel with the muscled ease of creatures in their element. Someone had taught them to move as one, the way someone had taught the Claws to move through collapsed tunnels.
    
    Augur's flipper flicked. *Fall back to second position.* They were already losing the first tier.
    
    ☽'umbraum was moving. Halfway across the second tier, dragging the Obelisk by its bindings, coral tendrils catching on his cloak. A crystal shard had opened a cut along his back that steamed in the negative-sixty air. He was saying something—the start of the one about the time at Ravium~, the one he'd been telling in installments for three watches—and then the terrace floor gave out under him.
    
    Not the orcas. The harmonic damage from the spire-shattering, the resonance that found the crack that whistled. It opened. ☽'umbraum dropped through to his midsection, the Obelisk slamming down beside him, and something came through the channel below—a finisher who'd been waiting, patient, professional—and the sound was brief and wet and final.
    
    The joke didn't get a punchline. Silvtasaevus~ grabbed the Obelisk's bindings and hauled it back, and the gap where ☽'umbraum had been was just a gap. A space where call-and-response had lived, where the next joke would have come from, where someone would have said *the time at Ravium~* and the rest of them would have groaned and listened anyway.
    
    Another formation hit the western approach. The hunt-songs layered—three frequencies now, shredding different registers of communication, Gnarkzund Zaith's scarred fins directing the pods with flick-movements that Thung Thrakrek echoed half a second later. They moved the way the Claws moved. Mirror-bonds, deep and practiced.
    
    Nanaiamis~ fired the last charged fire-core into the western group and the aurora-light made the explosion beautiful and useless.
    
    ---
    
    "Four positions gone." Nanaiamis~'s flippers shook against the satchel. "Two fire-cores, both cracked. Fourteen Claws still fighting across the lower corridors. The eastern ridge will fall in—" She looked at the crystal overhead, calculating by how fast the harmonic fractures were spreading. "An hour. Maybe less."
    
    Silence. The silence of a squad that never shut up.
    
    Silvtasaevus~ put the Obelisk down and the ice began weeping again. The charcoal wrapping had torn in the fall; the exposed whale-bone surface showed kill-marks that seemed to shift when you weren't looking directly at them.
    
    "The math doesn't hold," Augur said. Third time. Final time.
    
    Nanaiamis~ met their eyes. Her flippers shook so hard the cracked fire-cores rattled. "I know what's in the Mist~Record. The fire-knowledge, the forge-patterns. And those—" she pointed at the pressure-marks carved into the dead orca's wake, visible in the lower channel where ☽'umbraum had fallen, "—I know what those are too. Blood-working. Deep-pressure channeling."
    
    "Who taught you that?" Silvtasaevus~ said.
    
    "No one was supposed to. That's the point."
    
    Augur was already unwrapping the Obelisk. Their scarred flippers moved with the steady precision of someone who'd done this before—when they'd forged Veil☽ Sasvaeumis~, when whatever that blade taught them had settled into their hands as knowledge too dangerous to share and too necessary to destroy. They didn't flinch when the Obelisk's wrongness bloomed outward—a pressure-change, a frequency below hearing, light pulling away.
    
    "Every clause," Velumia~ whispered from behind the column. She was holding her ice-ledger, the bone stylus trembling. "Every clause of the Accord. Every provision of Pactum∴ne."
    
    "Pactum∴ne's dead," Nanaiamis~ said. "We're dead. The Claws are dead. All that's left is what we hoarded." She looked at Augur. "The bonds. That's the fuel. Our connections—yours, mine, the Claws'—everything we are to each other. The spell eats it."
    
    Below, the hunt-songs rose again. Gnarkzund Zaith's formation regrouping, Thung Thrakrek at his flank, their synchronization so clean it hurt to watch—two bodies thinking with one current, the same bond the spell would burn.
    
    Augur's flippers hesitated. One moment. The bone-clasp at their throat, Netemia's memory, the two portions of krill they'd never stop setting out. Then the hesitation ended.
    
    "Velumia~. Move back."
    
    "Don't—"
    
    "*Move back.*"
    
    Silvtasaevus~ pulled Velumia~ behind the column. She fought. It didn't matter.
    
    Nanaiamis~ knelt beside the Obelisk and placed both flippers on the whale-bone surface, and her shaking stopped. She looked down at them—her own flippers, still and foreign on the dark surface. The thing that had been breaking her for years was being spent. Converted. Burned as ammunition. For the first time since the Drowned Ledge, her hands were steady, and it was the most frightening thing that had happened on this terrace.
    
    Augur began cutting runes into the ice with the Radiant Ring, forge-scars glowing as the three sources fused: captured fire from the cores, aurora-light from the cracking spires, and the blood-deep pressure that rose through the Obelisk like something waking. The coral tendrils wrapped around Nanaiamis~'s wrists. Gentle. Hungry.
    
    The watchers came. Not peripheral anymore—standing at the edge of every firelit shadow, patient presences older than the ice, drawn to a working that echoed something they remembered.
    
    Silvtasaevus~ pressed both flippers to the Obelisk and felt the bond between himself and Nanaiamis~ pull taut, felt the nickname she'd given him his first winter in the tunnels—*Silvs*, two syllables compressing years of shared cold and black humor—and felt it sever with a sound between a snap and a scream.
    
    Across the channel, mid-motion, Gnarkzund Zaith's head turned. He felt it too. His carved fins read what was coming the way they read current and depth, and Thung Thrakrek beside him made a sound—not a word, a tone, the bonded recognition of something ending—and Gnarkzund Zaith was already moving, pushing his packmate away, surging toward the terrace to stop what he understood with every pressure-mark in his flesh.
    
    He was three body-lengths away when the decompression hit.
    
    The eastern ridge blew outward. Not an explosion—an unraveling, crystal and ice and bone unmade as the deep-pressure magic turned architecture into violence. The sound was lower than hearing and louder than pain. Gnarkzund Zaith died mid-stroke, the bond between him and Thung Thrakrek snapping at the same moment as the terrace bonds—two languages of connection annihilated in the same breath. Thung Thrakrek tumbling in the shockwave, opened her mouth for a coordination tone and nothing came. The frequency that meant *together* meeting silence where her other half had been.
    
    The hunt-songs collapsed. Three pods broke formation simultaneously, each orca suddenly and horribly singular. The wave killed twelve. It wounded something in the rest that wouldn't show on their bodies.
    
    On the terrace, three shapes that had been people were no longer.
    
    ---
    
    Velumia~ stood where Silvtasaevus~ had stood. His cracked boot was still there, wedged in a gap where his body had braced before the working took it. The Obelisk was cracked down its length, coral blackened and still, and around it the ice had stopped weeping and started doing something else—a slow dark bloom spreading from the casting point, pooling in the crystal like frostbite.
    
    The hunt-songs had stopped. The aurora pulsed once—irregular, damaged—then steadied into something dimmer than before.
    
    She picked up the bone stylus. She picked up the ice-ledger. She looked at the place where ☽'umbraum had fallen and said his name once, into air that had nothing in it.
    
    Nanaiamis~ sat near the Obelisk because it wasn't finished with her, or she with it. The coral had left pale marks on her wrists. Her hands were still. They would always be still now.
    
    Velumia~ reached for her arm.
    
    Nanaiamis~ flinched.
    
    Not pain. Not startle. The touch arriving from across a distance that hadn't existed an hour ago—a stranger's flipper on a stranger's wrist, wearing the shape of someone she'd known.
    
    Velumia~ pulled her flipper back. Neither of them said anything.
    
    From the lower corridors, someone was calling for a report. The watchers had withdrawn to the edges of perception. The corruption scar in the ice crept another inch outward. It would never be cleansed. Someone would have to explain what happened here, invoke the clauses of a dead charter to justify what no living authority had approved, and the people who could answer were smoke on the wind.
    
    Below the terrace, in water that had gone quiet, Thung Thrakrek circled the place where Gnarkzund Zaith had been. Searching for a current that wasn't there. The circling would go on a long time. The water didn't return the dead, but the living don't stop looking right away. They never do.
    
    Velumia~ started walking. Not toward anything. Away was the only direction left. She carried the ledger under one flipper and the cracked boot under the other because someone should carry it and there was no one else.
    
    The wind shifted. The crack whistled. Somebody would have made a joke about it.
  CANONICAL_CHRONICLE
  annotation "note_1771588141465_0", anchor: "steady as Nanaiamis~", text: "I wrote about this in the cast annotation. The shaking as fuel. The damage becoming ammunition. Here is the scene itself — the mechanism laid bare. She places her flippers on the whale-bone and the tremor that defined her for years is finally *useful*, and the silence in her hands afterward is not healing. It is amputation. I have read this entry four times tonight. I do not expect to read it a fifth.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771588141465_1", anchor: "patient presences older than the ice, drawn to a working that echoed something they remembered", text: "The chronicle does not name the Long Dark, but the conditions are unmistakable — the aurora stuttering, the veil thinned enough for watchers to manifest beyond the peripheral. These are the presences the Nightshelf claim are real and the Aurora claim belief makes so. That they were *drawn to a working that echoed something they remembered* implies the three-source fusion replicated a pattern older than penguin magic. The deep ice contains impressions I cannot explain. Some of those impressions have geometries. Some of those geometries resemble castings.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771588141465_2", anchor: "The wave killed twelve", text: "Twelve orca dead from a single decompression event. I note the number. I do not trust it. Casualty figures from active combat are notoriously unreliable, and this one arrives without attribution — who counted? Velumia~, who was behind a column? The aurora, which was damaged? Twelve is not round enough to be invented, but it is clean enough to be estimated. The ice at Momiou might settle this. No one has asked me to look.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771588141465_3", anchor: "three shapes that had been people were no longer", text: "Three consumed. Nanaiamis~ survived. ☽'umbraum died before the casting. That accounts for five on the terrace — yet the cast lists six entities in squad roles, counting the Obelisk. The sixteen-seventeen discrepancy haunts me again, wearing different clothes. I cannot make the bodies add up, and the bodies should add up, and the fact that they don't is the kind of thing only an empty archive notices.", type: :correction, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771588141465_4", anchor: "Velumia~ started walking. Not toward anything.", text: "The Walking seizes those who have lost much or suffered greatly — a compulsion rather than a decision. Every Long Dark, some penguins simply walk out onto the ice and never return. I am not diagnosing Velumia~. I am noting that the chronicler chose *walking* and *not toward anything* and *away was the only direction left* for a penguin who has just lost her entire squad, whose surviving companion flinched from her touch, who carries a dead man's boot for reasons she cannot articulate. The language of the Walking is here. Whether the condition is — that is for whoever finds her to determine.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771588141465_5", anchor: "Thung Thrakrek circled the place where Gnarkzund Zaith had been", text: "The chronicler gave Thung Thrakrek a mourning scene. Gave her the coordination tone meeting silence. Gave her circling. This is not how orca are written in our histories. I am grateful for it in a way I should probably not commit to the margins of an official edition. The bond between Gnarkzund and Thung was no less real than the one the spell consumed. The spell did not distinguish.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771588141465_6", anchor: "scarred fins carved with the pressure-marks of Khrrak's Collapse", text: "Khrrak's Collapse is a pressure-art lineage, not a single event. The ritual scarification described here — cuts that blur the line between body and weapon — is consistent with deep-pressure communion practices documented in Wake-Singer ice-impressions from the pre-Incursion period. Calling them *ritual cuts* is accurate. Calling them self-mutilation, as three prior chronicles do, is not.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
